# Phrase substitutions applied to the carved tree, after file selection and before
# the patches.
#
# ⚠ IDENTIFIERS AND PATHS ONLY. A substitution table does not know grammar. Used on
# prose it silently produced "a caller the caller asks", "the passive algorithms
# on-disk schema", a lower-case sentence opening, and a dangling "the other" with no
# antecedent -- each of them shipped, because a replacement that fires cleanly reports
# success whatever nonsense it leaves behind. Every sentence-level edit now lives in
# patches/, where a wrong assumption fails loudly instead of reading oddly.
#
# WHY THEY EXIST AT ALL: `ConMin` in the development repository is not a rival paper's
# coined term, it is the name of a package beside ConGen, and a comment pointing at a
# sibling module is correct documentation there. It becomes wrong only in the artifact,
# which does not ship that module -- there the name points at something absent, exactly
# like a stale file path. So the fix belongs on the artifact side.
#
# ORDER MATTERS: rules apply in file order, so a longer phrase must precede any shorter
# phrase contained in it, or the short rule consumes the text and the long one never
# fires. The match-at-least-once assertion is what surfaces that.
#
# FORMAT: one `find<TAB>replace` per line. Blank lines and # comments ignored.
# Every entry must match at least once, and no `find` may survive: carve.sh asserts
# both, and refuses any rule that would touch data/.

